          <h2>What stands out</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>esbuild has 201M weekly downloads. One maintainer.</strong> Evan Wallace built one of the most
            important tools in the JavaScript ecosystem &mdash; the bundler that powers Vite, Next.js, and dozens
            of other frameworks. It&rsquo;s exceptional engineering. It&rsquo;s also a single point of failure
            for roughly half the JavaScript build toolchain. If something happens to Evan&rsquo;s npm token,
            the blast radius is enormous.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>That&rsquo;s more downloads than TypeScript (178M/wk).</strong> TypeScript has 6 maintainers.
            esbuild has 1.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sharp processes images on ~55M npm installs per week.</strong> One maintainer. Server-side
            image processing for most Node.js production deployments. It has native bindings. A malicious version
            would be hard to detect and devastating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Chalk (412M downloads/week) is still the biggest exposure.</strong> The most downloaded package
            on npm that&rsquo;s sole-maintained. It colors your terminal output. Every project that has a CLI, every
            build script, every logging framework &mdash; chalk is in there. One token compromise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The &ldquo;safe&rdquo; packages earn it.</strong> webpack (score=100) has 8 maintainers, 44M
            weekly downloads, and 15 years of shipping. prettier has 11 maintainers. TypeScript is Microsoft-backed.
            These packages would survive a maintainer leaving. The CRITICAL packages wouldn&rsquo;t.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The ua-parser-js attack (October 2021) proved the model.</strong> A compromised npm token published
            malicious versions in minutes. <code>npm audit</code> showed zero issues beforehand. The structural
            profile had been visible for years: 1 maintainer, millions of downloads/week = prime target.
          </p>

          <h2>Why this matters now</h2>

          <p>Three patterns converged in early 2026:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>AI-assisted supply chain attacks are getting faster.</strong> Identifying a high-value target
              (1 maintainer + massive downloads), generating a plausible malicious payload, and timing the publish
              to a token compromise &mdash; all of this can be automated.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>npm audit waits for CVEs.</strong> The database catches known vulnerabilities. It has nothing
              to say about structural risk. Both tools answer different questions. You need both.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Transitive dependencies hide the risk.</strong> I audited <code>@anthropic-ai/sdk</code> &mdash;
              score=86, 14 maintainers, looks solid. But two levels deep: <code>json-schema-to-ts</code> (CRITICAL,
              sole maintainer, 12M downloads/week). You&rsquo;d never find that in a direct audit.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h2>How to run it yourself</h2>

          <pre><code># Zero install — try it now
npx proof-of-commitment axios zod chalk hono esbuild

# Against your own project
npx proof-of-commitment --file package.json

# Scan ALL transitive dependencies (lock file)
npx proof-of-commitment --file package-lock.json

# PyPI too
npx proof-of-commitment --pypi litellm langchain requests</code></pre>

